; Nuke — the surface language's syntax layer.
;
; This file is a fragment. The normative grammar of the surface language is
; `grammar/tokens.abnf` followed by this file, in that order. The token layer is
; shared with the canonical form, so it is defined there and not here.
;
; The two syntax layers are never read together, so both name their rules the
; same way and neither may be assembled with the other. Three rules below —
; `field`, `entry` and `list` — read word for word like the canonical form's,
; and they are not the same rules, because the `value` under them is this one.
;
; A document is valid when it matches `document` and every `number` token it
; contains also matches `surface-number`, which is where this language stops
; spelling a number the way the canonical form does.

; A document is one value, and a file whose value is a tuple may leave the braces
; to the file. What stands after the bindings is then either one value or a run
; of fields, and the boundary of the text is what `{` and `}` would have been —
; which is Rust's arrangement, where `mod foo { … }` and `foo.rs` are one thing
; and the file is what replaces the braces. `docs/imports.md` already said a
; file's bindings are private and its fields are its surface; braceless, that is
; true of the text rather than of a tuple the author had to write.
;
; It is `1*field` and not `*field`, so `{}` keeps its one spelling and a file
; that only binds names is still no document. A map has no braceless spelling: a
; field run is decided after one identifier, where an entry could not be told
; from a value until a whole value had been read and an `=>` found past it.
;
; `fields` stands ahead of `value` because an alternation is ordered once
; translated, and `value` would match the `x` of `x = 1` as a reference and stop
; there. Nothing is taken away by the order — a document beginning `ident ows
; "="` cannot be read as a value today either, so this rule only ever fires
; where the other one had already failed.
;
; No `ows` leads `fields`. The document's own leading `ows`, or the one after
; the last binding, has already run, which is why this is not the tuple's
; `*( ows field )`.

document   = ows *( binding ows ) ( fields / value ) ows
fields     = field *( ows field )

; `<|` applies a function to an argument, and `|>` pipes an argument into one.
; Application is an operator rather than juxtaposition, and that is forced: a
; collection carries no separators, so `[f a b]` could not tell a call of two
; arguments from a call beside a value, and `[f (x + 1)]` could not tell an
; application from two elements. What stood here before was one rule that
; hardcoded the function — a builtin, named after a sigil — into the operator
; itself, and splitting the two is what gives a function that is no builtin
; somewhere to stand.
;
; Neither spelling spends a character. Arithmetic is owed `+ - * / %`, comparison
; `< > = !`, and — since a language without reserved words cannot spell `and`,
; `or` or `not` as words — conjunction is owed `& | !` too; and `( )` is owed to
; grouping, which `group` below now spends it on. A digraph draws on none of it:
; `|` stays owed to disjunction and `<` and `>` to comparison, because `a | > b`
; was never anything and so `|>` steals no reading from it. `^` and `~` are left
; unspent, which is what a digraph is for.
;
; `<|` is the loosest operator there is, and that is its job: it eats the
; parenthesis standing to its right, where Haskell's `$` sits for the same
; reason. So `@concat <| xs |> @flatten` is a concat of what the pipe yields. It
; is right-associative because `f <| g <| x` has only one useful reading, and
; `|>` is left-associative because a pipeline is read in the order it is written.
;
; Both operands carry their access chains, which settles a bill this file has
; owed since a call took an `operand` rather than a `value`: `f <| p.a` now reads
; as it looks, `(f <| p).a` takes the parenthesis grouping was always owed, and
; `@concat <| theme.parts` has a spelling where before it had none.
;
; Two directions are not two spellings of one thing. `@concat <| xs` and
; `xs |> @concat` do reduce alike and no tool prefers either — the bargain a file
; standing in for its own braces already struck — but a chain reads one way and a
; call reads the other, and neither is the readable one in both places.

value      = apply
apply      = pipe [ ows "<|" ows apply ]
pipe       = projection *( ows "|>" ows projection )

; Projection reads a value out of a collection. It is postfix rather than
; recursive on its left because a PEG admits no left recursion, and it takes
; `ows` on both sides because every other operator does — so `[a .b]` holds one
; element, not two. It binds tighter than either application operator, which is
; what leaves `f <| p.a` reading as the projection it looks like.
;
; There is one operator and two right operands. A name is taken literally and
; projects a tuple's field; a group is evaluated and its value is the key an
; entry is read at. That is the whole rule — `p.accent` is the field,
; `m.("accent")` and `m.(k)` are the entry — and it is what keeps `.a` from
; becoming a second spelling of `.("a")`.
;
; The parenthesis is not a character spent. Grouping was owed `( )` from the
; moment an operator could exist, and what stands after the dot is exactly a
; group, so this rule spent nothing and `m.(1 + 1)` is already what it says. The
; square bracket was the other
; candidate and is refused: `[ ]` means list everywhere else here, so `m.["a"]`
; reads as though a list were being projected. Postfix `m["a"]` is refused for
; a harder reason — collections carry no separators, so `[a [1]]` would be read
; as one projection and a list holding a name beside a list would have no
; spelling at all.
;
; It is also where the token layer's greediness finally shows: a number token
; takes the point with it, so `1.b` is the malformed number `1.` beside a name,
; `1.(k)` is that number beside a group, and only `1 . b` is a projection.
; Nothing new is at work — `[12]` and `[1 2]` differ for the same reason — but
; `.` is the first thing that is not a value and can still run together with one.

projection = operand *( ows access )
access     = "." ows ( ident / group )

; `@` names a builtin, and `import` is the first of them. It is a sigil and not
; the word `import` for the reason `:=` is not `let` — the canonical form has no
; reserved words — and the sigil is what keeps it that way: `import` is a name
; only after `@`, so `import := 1` and `{import = 1}` stay legal and unrelated.
; Which builtins exist is not a question a grammar can answer, so `builtin` names
; none; an unknown one is a fault the evaluator raises.
;
; `@` reads as "at", which is what a path is, and it now says that and nothing
; more — the name, never the call. A builtin is an operand like any other and
; stands wherever one does, but it is not yet a *value*: `Value` is the canonical
; form and admits no seventh spelling, so a `@name` nothing applies is a fault
; the evaluator raises as well. That restriction lifts the day functions arrive,
; and no spelling changes when it does.
;
; `ows` stands on both sides of the name because every other operator takes it,
; and here it is free: `@` begins and continues no other token, so unlike `.`
; there is no greedy token to collide with and nothing to argue.

; A group is one value in parentheses, and it is what pays for an operator: with
; `<|` binding loosest of all, `(f <| p).a` is the only way to project out of
; what an application yields, and `f <| (p.a)` the only way to spell the reading
; the dot no longer takes. `access` has held this rule since it needed a key, and
; nothing about it changes here except where it may stand.
;
; It could not have stood here before. `[f (x)]` is two elements only because a
; parenthesis opening a value cannot also continue one, and that is true exactly
; while juxtaposition means nothing — which is what an application operator
; settles. Grouping and `<|` are one decision, not two.
;
; A group is a shape and not a step: `(1)` and `1` reduce alike, and it survives
; parsing only so that a document reprints as its author spelled it.

operand    = group / builtin / interpolation / tuple / map / list / reference
           / atom / string / number
group      = "(" ows value ows ")"
builtin    = "@" ows ident

; `$"a{expr}b"` interpolates. The prefix is a sigil and not Python's `f` because
; tokens are greedy and `f` and `"a"` do not run together into one of them: today
; `[f "a"]` and `[f"a"]` are the same two elements, and gluing an identifier to a
; following string would break the rule that whitespace is required only where a
; token would otherwise continue. `$` begins no token at all, so `$"` glues with
; nothing to argue and `$ "a"` stays two things, neither of which is a value.
;
; A hole lives here and never in `string`, which the token layer shares with the
; canonical form: a canonical string may hold any text, so a sigil given meaning
; inside `"…"` would change what some canonical document already means. Nothing
; crosses the other way — an interpolation reduces to an ordinary string, and the
; canonical form gains no rule from this one.
;
; `{{` and `}}` are the literal braces, which is Rust's spelling and not `\{`
; because `escape` belongs to the shared token layer: widening it would leave the
; two languages disagreeing about what an escape is, and confining the rule to
; `doubled` leaves it untouched. A lone `}` is therefore a fault rather than a
; brace, the one spelling a plain string keeps and this one does not.
;
; A hole takes a `value` rather than an `operand`, the braces having already done
; the grouping a call cannot, so `{p.a}` reads the way it looks. `ows` stands
; inside them like it stands inside every other bracket, which is also how a
; tuple reaches a hole: `{{` is a brace, so `{ {a = 1}.a }` needs its space.
;
; `:` opens a format specifier, spelled as Rust spells it. `ows` stands before it
; as it stands before every operator and behind it never, which is the one place
; this language is whitespace sensitive outside a string — a fill character may be
; a space, so `{a: >8}` pads with them and `{a:>8 }` is a specifier that does not
; end. The specifier is a raw tail running to the `}` that closes the hole.
;
; A width is a `uint` rather than a run of digits, so `{n:007}` is refused for the
; reason `[01]` is, and it is refused here rather than in Rust's company.
;
; `spec` admits the empty string and the parser does not, `{a}` being the one
; spelling of a hole with nothing asked of it. What is missing against Rust is
; missing on purpose: `?` would mean "spell this as Nuke syntax", which is what
; a formatter does and not what an evaluator does, and `w$` points into an
; argument list that a language putting expressions in its holes has not got.

interpolation = %s"$" DQUOTE *piece DQUOTE
piece         = literal / hole
literal       = 1*( plain / escape / doubled )
plain         = %x20-21 / %x23-5B / %x5D-7A / %x7C / %x7E-10FFFF
doubled       = "{{" / "}}"
hole          = "{" ows value ows [ ":" spec ] "}"

spec          = [ aligned ] [ "+" ] [ "#" ] [ "0" ] [ uint ] [ "." uint ]
                [ notation ]
aligned       = ( fill align ) / align
align         = "<" / "^" / ">"
fill          = %x20-21 / %x23-7A / %x7C / %x7E-10FFFF
notation      = %s"b" / %s"o" / %s"x" / %s"X" / %s"e"

; A binding names a value and contributes nothing to the result. It stands at
; the head of the document or of a brace block, before any pair. A list holds
; values and a binding is not one, so a list holds none.

binding    = ident ows ":=" ows value
reference  = ident

tuple      = "{" *( ows binding ) *( ows field ) ows "}"
field      = ident ows "=" ows value

map        = "{" *( ows binding ) *( ows entry ) ows "}"
entry      = value ows "=>" ows value

list       = "[" *( ows value ) ows "]"

; A dyadic literal spells an integer as a bit string. A marker says how many
; bits each digit after it carries — `b` one, `q` two, `o` three, `x` four — so
; a segment is a marker and its digits, and the literal is the segments run
; together: `0b101110100xC` is nine bits of binary and then four of hex. Ten is
; no power of two, so there is no decimal marker and that is what dyadic means.
;
; `hexdig` is the token layer's and is uppercase, which is what lets `b`, `q`,
; `o` and `x` be markers everywhere, `0xF35b1` included. It is also why there is
; no digit separator: a marker repeated is one, `0xDEADxBEEF` being `0xDEADBEEF`.
;
; The leading `0` carries no bits. It is there so that a number still starts
; with a digit, which is what tells a lexer to look for one.
;
; `dyadic` stands first for the reason `dyadic-run` does: an alternation is
; ordered once translated, and `canonical-number` would match the `0` of `0xFF`
; and stop there.

surface-number = dyadic / canonical-number
dyadic         = [ "-" ] "0" 1*segment
segment        = ( %s"b" 1*bindig ) / ( %s"q" 1*quatdig )
               / ( %s"o" 1*octdig ) / ( %s"x" 1*hexdig )
bindig         = %x30-31
quatdig        = %x30-33
octdig         = %x30-37
